"""Integrate verified R3 additions into a preserved R2 LaTeX baseline."""
from pathlib import Path

root=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
src=root/'working_R3/source'
s=(root/'baseline_source/main.tex').read_text()
start=s.index('\\begin{abstract}')
end=s.index('\\end{abstract}',start)
abstract=r'''\begin{abstract}
We establish training-derived conditions that certify or exclude nearest-prototype agreement in Gaussian radial basis function classifiers over continuous bandwidth--penalty regions. A class-wise residual decomposition, prototype-block spectral floor, and exact penalty envelope certify complete regions $(0,t]\times[\lambda_0,\infty)$. A strict competing vote in the large-penalty limit supplies a finite disagreement bound, and a signed interval construction certifies local bandwidth bands with finite penalty floors. A frozen MNIST and Fashion-MNIST study uses five seeds and 50--200 prototypes; subsequent diagnostics retain all ten 100-prototype groups. Immediately beyond the sufficient frontier, more than 99.5\% of query--region pairs remain unresolved. At $t=s_C$, where $s_C$ is the median interprototype distance divided by $\sqrt{2}$, the original witness union excludes 88.10\% and 84.48\% of queries, respectively. Local bands $[0.99t,t]$ at $t=s_C/2$ certify 16.88\% and 40.09\% at a penalty floor $10\|A_t\|_2$, where $A_t$ is the endpoint feature Gram matrix. The certified subsets achieve 69.09\% and 75.27\% endpoint accuracy; matched-coverage agreement-gated distance selection achieves 85.55\% and 79.34\%. At widths with competitive sampled models, every finite sufficient reversal bound exceeds the largest qualifying penalty at the same width. These results characterize the geometry and empirical reach of continuous decision persistence, distinguish missing sufficient guarantees from identified incompatibility, and quantify how analytical exclusions complement finite-model checks.
'''
s=s[:start]+abstract+s[end:]
s=s.replace('Validation partitions are retained as disjoint unused partitions; the present study performs no model selection.', 'In the frozen study, validation partitions were retained as disjoint unused partitions and no model selection was performed. Section~\\ref{sec:r3-design} subsequently uses those partitions for a post hoc diagnostic of archived readouts.')
old='Focused post hoc analyses measure the exclusion partition and local certificate on the same 100-prototype groups, reporting region geometry, coverage, fitted agreement, and class accuracy separately.'
new=old+' Follow-up comparisons locate sufficient reversal bounds relative to validation-based competitive models, measure exact overlap with all archived finite witnesses, and compare certified subsets with informed selectors at identical coverage. These comparisons connect the parameter-family guarantees to the behavior and accuracy of finite readouts.'
assert old in s;s=s.replace(old,new)
s=s.replace('\\input{new_methods.tex}','\\input{new_methods.tex}\n\\input{r3_methods.tex}')
s=s.replace('\\input{new_results.tex}','\\input{new_results.tex}\n\\input{r3_results.tex}')
s=s.replace('Appendix~\\ref{app:exact-ridge-examples}','\\ref{app:exact-ridge-examples}')
old='The finite threshold in Proposition~\\ref{prop:ridge-limit} makes this mechanism a statement about actual finite classifiers.'
new='The finite threshold in Proposition~\\ref{prop:ridge-limit} makes this mechanism a statement about actual finite classifiers. The validation comparison places these sufficient bounds far beyond the competitive sampled penalties. It locates the guaranteed reversal along the path, with the first empirical crossing remaining a separate quantity.'
assert old in s;s=s.replace(old,new)
old='The measured coverage at broader widths establishes that this region geometry can certify additional nearest-label behavior. The fitted large-penalty readouts have low all-query accuracy in the reported conditions. These outcomes identify the scientific use of the extension as analysis of decision persistence across parameter regions. Coverage and class accuracy answer different questions and are reported with their own denominators.'
new='The measured coverage at broader widths establishes that this region geometry can certify additional nearest-label behavior. The matched-coverage experiment places its selected-set accuracy above random selection and ordinary score margins. Agreement-gated distance selection is more accurate in three of the four comparisons. The MNIST endpoint at $t=s_C$ and $\\lambda=10\\norm{A_t}_2$ has a constant class-1 readout, and its certified set equals the entire endpoint-agreement pool. These measurements identify the positive analytical result as a guarantee of persistence across a continuous band and penalty tail, with selected-set composition determining the observed accuracy.'
assert old in s;s=s.replace(old,new)
anchor='The original bound comparisons explain which information matters within the full-width construction.'
added='Exact overlap analysis further identifies the source of incremental exclusions. Near $t=s_C$, the original limiting witnesses add many cases to the original finite probes, and almost all of those additions are covered once the archived local-tail models are included. At $2s_C$, limiting witnesses retain a larger unique set on Fashion-MNIST, where the archive has fewer high-penalty finite models. Complementarity is therefore a measurable relation between the analytical witness and a specified finite sampling design. The limiting calculation provides a training-derived route to the exclusion; finite readouts can expose many of the same disagreements.\n\n'
assert anchor in s;s=s.replace(anchor,added+anchor)
old='The two-dataset evidence distinguishes the early loss of sufficient certification from widespread broad-width incompatibility, while demonstrating positive local-tail coverage at large penalties. The resulting contribution is a training-derived analysis of decision persistence with certified, excluded, and unresolved outputs. Further work can target the unresolved frontier with tighter signed bounds and evaluate how region geometry changes under other prototype selections.'
new='The two-dataset evidence distinguishes the early loss of sufficient certification from widespread broad-width incompatibility and establishes positive local-tail coverage at large penalties. Validation comparisons locate the sufficient reversals beyond the competitive sampled penalties. Exact witness unions quantify the information added to finite-model checks, and matched-coverage selectors identify the role of class composition in certified-subset accuracy. The resulting contribution is a training-derived analysis of decision persistence with certified, excluded, and unresolved outputs. Tighter signed bounds and alternative region geometries provide concrete routes for studying the unresolved frontier.'
assert old in s;s=s.replace(old,new)
old='The new diagnostic and local-tail analyses are post hoc and use the ten $K=100$ groups.'
new='The diagnostic, local-tail, validation-reference, and selector analyses are post hoc and use the ten $K=100$ groups. The 95\\% competitive rule describes the archived validation grid, and the sufficient reversal bound supplies an upper threshold for guaranteed disagreement rather than an estimate of its first onset. The selector comparisons describe the fixed endpoint models and sample; a predictive or model-selection benefit at competitive penalties remains an open empirical requirement.'
assert old in s;s=s.replace(old,new)
s=s.replace('The additional diagnostic and local-tail protocols, code, per-query records, new fitted readouts, and independent synthetic checks are provided in the accompanying revision supplement.', 'The additional diagnostic and local-tail protocols, code, per-query records, fitted readouts, and independent synthetic checks are provided in the accompanying revision supplement. It also includes the follow-up validation predictions, sufficient-reversal distributions, exact witness-overlap masks, matched-cardinality selection records, class distributions, and independent numerical reconstruction.')
s=s.replace('\\begingroup\\raggedright\n\\bibliography{references}', '\\begingroup\\raggedright\n\\setlength{\\bibsep}{2pt}\n\\bibliography{references}')
(src/'main.tex').write_text(s)
nr=(root/'baseline_source/new_results.tex').read_text()
old='For the 1\\% bands, certified-subset accuracies are 69.09\\% and 75.27\\% at $s_C/2$, and 68.93\\% and 64.65\\% at $s_C$. The new regions establish computable label agreement at broader widths and identify its penalty-dependent accuracy tradeoff. Together with the exclusion analysis, they characterize which classifier families can share the prototype summary.'
new='Section~\\ref{sec:r3-selectors} evaluates the certified-subset accuracies against matched-coverage selectors and reports the selected class distributions. The new regions establish computable label agreement at broader widths and identify its penalty-dependent accuracy tradeoff.'
assert old in nr;nr=nr.replace(old,new)
(src/'new_results.tex').write_text(nr)
print('Integrated main.tex and new_results.tex from immutable R2 baseline.')
